WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.450
- [Narrator] The economy is in
a confusing place right now,

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.020
and there's a lot of speculation
around this one word.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.420
- The latest sign that we may
be headed for a recession.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.160
- Are we actually in a recession?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.800
- I do not think the US is
currently in a recession.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.300
- [Narrator] So what actually determines

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.100
when the US enters a recession?

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.240
It's not this or this or this.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.760
No one chart determines a recession.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.560
Rather it's these eight people,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.470
a group known as the Business
Cycle Dating Committee.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.880
Here's how this group decides

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.210
when the US is in a recession
and why knowing that date

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.208
is so important.

00:00:34.208 --> 00:00:36.791
(gentle music)

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.210
- It matters if you're
gonna lose your job.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.730
It matters if your income is going down.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.110
It matters to business

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:50.010
if they're gonna be selling
fewer cars or fewer televisions.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.120
So our entire economy is
oriented towards producing more,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.580
and a recession is when
everything goes wrong

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.110
and we go in the other direction.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.540
- [Narrator] Knowing when
a recession starts and ends

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.997
is important, because it
can inform how we respond

00:01:02.997 --> 00:01:05.190
to these downturns later.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.520
This group of economists
is selected by the NBER,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.370
or National Bureau of Economic Research.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.140
Each member on the committee is an expert,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.870
in well, business cycle dating.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.320
Business cycles are patterns
of peaks, or high points,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.680
and troughs, low points,
in economic activity.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.770
That period between a peak
and a trough is a recession.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.540
The NBER has been dating business cycles

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.960
back to at least the 1920s,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.750
but formalized the committee in 1978.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.340
The committee is nonpartisan

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.830
and its makeup doesn't
follow set guidelines.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.750
The number of people on it can vary,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.330
and some have been there
since its inception.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.580
It's included people
like former Fed Chairman,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.570
Ben Bernanke.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:46.800
- Thank you.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.570
- What they try to do is seek balance

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:52.080
in a really even handed,
data only approach

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.300
to just looking at the
data and what they show

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.250
and put the politics out of it.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.930
- [Narrator] The committee
doesn't meet regularly,

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.480
only when economic data looks
like it might be reaching

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.740
a peak or trough.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.620
So what are the data
they're actually looking at?

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.170
The NBER officially defines a recession

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.921
as a decline in economic activity

00:02:08.921 --> 00:02:10.860
that's spread across the economy

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.080
and lasts more than a few months.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.910
To track that economic activity,

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.460
the committee looks at several indicators,

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.430
income, employment, real sales,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.320
personal consumption expenditures,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.140
or the value of stuff
bought by people in the US,

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.850
and industrial production.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.770
- What they try to do
is they try to look at

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:33.770
broad indicators that measure
all of the economy at once.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.160
- [Narrator] Across these categories,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.990
they're looking for three things,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.140
depth, which is how far
indicators are declining,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.960
diffusion, which is how
far the decline has spread

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.240
across the economy, and duration,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.550
which is how long those declines last.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.880
If each shows deep declines
lasting a long time,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.370
that would mark a clear
start to a recession.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.230
Just take a look at 2007,

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.230
when all of these categories
saw a significant drop

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.770
after December, but it's
not always so clear cut.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.500
- The economy is this dynamic organism,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.320
and it doesn't always behave
according to the textbooks

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.200
or the conventions about how
we think it should behave.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.880
- [Narrator] The time
that each indicator peaks

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.470
can vary widely.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.990
This chart shows how many
months before or after

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.020
a recession start date that
some of these reached a peak.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.200
For example, in 1980,
sales started to decline

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.200
10 months ahead of the
committee's economic peak.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.460
Or look at 2020,

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:27.720
when the committee called a recession

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.820
at the start of the COVID 19 pandemic,

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.940
even though the downturn
had only lasted two months

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.520
James Poterba, the president of the NBER

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.650
explained the reasoning on CNBC,

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.650
when the start of the recession
was initially declared.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.530
- The depth of this downturn is such that

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.940
the committee felt comfortable
labeling that as a recession.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.470
- Yet, the thing with these economists

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.970
is they wanna be scientific

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.950
but there's some art to this too.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.320
There's some kind of just
like feeling around a problem

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.870
and just saying, we
gotta make our best call.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.730
- [Narrator] This method
of calling a recession

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:00.990
isn't standard.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:04.800
Many analysts and commentators
across the globe look at GDP,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.390
or gross domestic product,

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.850
for guidance on when a
recession has started.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.519
GDP is a measure of how
much goods and services

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:13.320
and nation produces.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.020
The common rule of thumb
is that anytime it declines

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.440
in two consecutive quarters,
that indicates a recession.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.690
You can see that was the case in 1990,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.050
but again, this data alone
isn't enough because...

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:28.830
- The challenge with that is
that how much stuff we produce

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.410
as an economy doesn't
tell the whole story,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.240
it only tells part of this story.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.350
- If you look at 2022, GDP did
drop in these two quarters,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:38.310
but employment,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.310
a key indicator for the
committee was still strong.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.250
So the NBER didn't call it a recession,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.620
at least it hasn't yet.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:47.550
That's because the committee

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.700
usually doesn't call a recession
for months down the line.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.100
Just look at the start
of the 2008 recession,

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.210
which the committee didn't make

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.120
an official announcement about for a year.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.280
- The fact of the matter
is a lot of these data

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.670
that they're looking at are revised

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.800
months or years down the road.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.370
And so when they make these declarations,

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.560
they wanna be on firm ground,

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.480
they don't want to be revising the dates

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.140
that they put out there as
often as the data are revised.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.720
So they tend to act with
a little bit of a lag.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.880
- [Narrator] So when the
committee does make a call,

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.710
it doesn't often have that much effect

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.960
on the current economic situation.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.900
Still, knowing the precise
time a recession has started

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:29.580
is important politically

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.510
- When someone is running
for president or reelection,

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.700
people make judgments
about whether that person

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.340
has done a good job in office.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.520
And so we look at whether
there was a recession

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.980
- [Narrator] And it's also
important economically.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.570
- By having that sharpened view,

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.430
they could kind of do some analysis of,

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.830
well, what were interest
rates doing around then?

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:50.993
What was inflation doing?

00:05:50.993 --> 00:05:53.010
What were jobs doing?

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:56.700
So by sharpening your view of the past,

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.500
the hope is you can make better judgements

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.550
about the present.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.020
- [Narrator] Though the
committee hasn't yet determined

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.820
if the US has entered a recession,

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.341
economists in a WSJ survey

00:06:04.341 --> 00:06:07.260
think we're likely
headed into one this year

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.910
- During and after the COVID shock,

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.790
there's just been a lot of
turbulence in this economy

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.580
and so we have indicators
going up and down,

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.830
in different divergent directions

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.630
that make it very hard to read

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.790
how the economy is behaving right now.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:22.800
- [Narrator] And since the
committee is backward looking,

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.950
there isn't a clear timeline on when or if

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:27.356
an announcement could come.

00:06:27.356 --> 00:06:29.939
(gentle music)

